Second Problem: “Merge” Sorted Arrays
This was another variation on a well-known problem, this time being on the LeetCode problem 88 External-Link-Alt.
The setup was simple: Given two long arrays, the elements within which were already sorted in non-decreasing
order, I had to perform some swaps in-place such that the minimum elements are held in the first array, and
the maximum in the second, while preserving the non-decreasing order across both arrays.
An example test case used was as follows:
a = [1, 2, 8, 10], b = [5, 6,
9, 11]
After running the algorithm, the arrays should have been:
a = [1, 2, 5,
6], b = [8, 9, 10, 11]
Page 5 of 12

There was also this other spanner thrown in by the interviewer that if a[0] < b[0], then a should be treated
as the “minimum” array, else b.
I initially picked a to be the “minimum” array, and ran a two-pointer nested loop across both arrays, swapping
bigger elements out to b in favor of smaller ones in a, while also correcting any internal inversions.
My implementation was certainly convoluted, but I justified every decision I took and explained the logic
aloud with test cases. This is the code I presented:
